\name{im.classify.timeseries}
\alias{im.classify.timeseries}
\title{Classify a Time Series of Raster Images Using Common K-Means Clustering}
\usage{
im.classify.timeseries(
  input_images,
  num_clusters = 3,
  seed = NULL,
  do_plot = TRUE,
  custom_colors = NULL,
  num_colors = 100
)
}
\arguments{
  \item{input_images}{A list of \code{SpatRaster} objects representing images
  acquired at different times. All images must have the same geometry and
  number of layers. At least two images are required.}

  \item{num_clusters}{Integer. Number of classes to identify using k-means
  clustering. Must be greater than or equal to 2. Defaults to 3.}

  \item{seed}{Optional integer used to ensure reproducibility of the
  k-means classification. Defaults to \code{NULL}.}

  \item{do_plot}{Logical. If \code{TRUE}, the classified images are
  automatically displayed in a multi-panel layout. Defaults to \code{TRUE}.}

  \item{custom_colors}{Optional character vector of at least two valid
  color names or hex codes used to generate a custom visualization palette.
  If \code{NULL}, the viridis palette is used. Defaults to \code{NULL}.}

  \item{num_colors}{Integer. Number of colors to interpolate in the
  visualization palette. Defaults to 100.}
}
\description{
Performs unsupervised classification of a time series of raster images
using a common k-means clustering model. By combining pixel observations
from all input images, the function ensures consistent class definitions
across different time steps, allowing direct comparison of spatial
patterns and class distributions over time.
}
\details{
The function extracts pixel values from all input raster images and
combines them into a single dataset before performing k-means clustering.
Unlike independent classifications performed separately on each image,
this approach produces a common classification model for the entire
time series.

All input images must have identical geometry and the same number of
layers. Pixel observations containing missing values in any layer are
excluded from the clustering procedure. These pixels are assigned
\code{NA} in the corresponding classified image.

The classification procedure consists of the following steps:

\enumerate{
  \item Checking the geometry and number of layers of all input images.
  \item Extracting complete pixel observations from each image.
  \item Combining valid observations into a single dataset.
  \item Performing k-means clustering on the combined dataset.
  \item Assigning cluster labels to the corresponding pixels in each image.
  \item Reconstructing classified raster images while preserving their geometry.
}

Since all images are classified using the same cluster centers, class
labels remain consistent throughout the time series. This allows
changes in class distributions and spatial patterns to be investigated
without inconsistencies caused by independent clustering procedures.

If \code{seed} is provided, the random initialization of k-means is
reproducible.

If \code{do_plot = TRUE}, the classified images are displayed in a
multi-panel layout using \code{im.multiframe()}. By default, the viridis
color palette is used. If \code{custom_colors} is provided, a custom
palette is generated by interpolating the specified colors.

The classification is based on spectral similarity rather than predefined
thematic categories. Therefore, the ecological interpretation of each
class should be based on the spectral characteristics of its cluster
center.

This function is particularly useful for monitoring vegetation dynamics,
detecting land-cover changes, and investigating spatial and temporal
ecological patterns using multispectral or other raster time series.
}
\value{
A list containing:

\itemize{
  \item \code{result}: A list of single-layer \code{SpatRaster} objects
  containing the classified images, with consistent class labels
  across time. Input list names are preserved when available.

  \item \code{centers}: A matrix containing the k-means cluster centers,
  representing the spectral values associated with each class.
}
}
\seealso{
\code{\link{im.classify}},
\code{\link{im.multiframe}},
\code{\link{im.barplot}}
}
\examples{
\dontrun{
library(terra)

# Create two example raster images with three spectral bands
r1 <- rast(nrows = 50, ncols = 50, nlyrs = 3)
values(r1) <- matrix(runif(ncell(r1) * 3), ncol = 3)

r2 <- rast(r1)
values(r2) <- matrix(runif(ncell(r2) * 3), ncol = 3)

# Organize images into a time series
images <- list(time1 = r1, time2 = r2)

# Perform common k-means classification
classified <- im.classify.timeseries(
  images,
  num_clusters = 3,
  seed = 42
)

# Access classified images
classified$result

# Access cluster centers
classified$centers

# Perform classification without automatic plotting
classified <- im.classify.timeseries(
  images,
  num_clusters = 4,
  seed = 42,
  do_plot = FALSE
)

# Use a custom color palette
classified <- im.classify.timeseries(
  images,
  num_clusters = 4,
  seed = 42,
  custom_colors = c("blue", "yellow", "red")
)

# Plot class distribution for the first image
im.barplot(classified$result[[1]], perc = TRUE)

# Compare class distributions across time
im.barplot(classified$result[[2]], perc = TRUE)
}
}
